          </div>
        </div>

        <nav class="toc-card" aria-label="Contents">
          <h2 class="toc-title">Contents</h2>
          <ul class="toc-list">
            <li><a href="#locked-oven">Locked Gorenje Oven – Why It Happens and How to Unlock It</a></li>
            <li><a href="#other-problems">Other Common Technical Problems with Gorenje Ovens</a></li>
            <li><a href="#home-checks">What You Can Safely Check at Home</a></li>
            <li><a href="#stop-using">When You Should Stop Using the Appliance Immediately</a></li>
            <li><a href="#professional-repair">Professional Gorenje Oven Repair in Sofia and Varna</a></li>
            <li><a href="#technician-visit">What to Expect During a Visit from a RotoRem Technician</a></li>
            <li><a href="#prevention">How to Prevent the Problem from Recurring</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
          </ul>
        </nav>

        <div class="content-grid">
          <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none article-content">
            <p>
              Gorenje ovens offer a variety of programmes and safety features, but errors may occur after prolonged use. Common signs of a problem include a locked door, an unresponsive control panel, an unfamiliar symbol on the display or a failure to heat up.
            </p>
            <p>
              These issues do not always indicate a serious technical fault. Sometimes, the cause is an activated safety feature, an unfinished programme or the need to reset the clock after a power cut. In other cases, a professional diagnosis is required to determine the exact cause.
            </p>
            <p>
              In this article, we will look at the possible reasons why a Gorenje oven may become locked, the safe checks you can perform at home, other common faults and the situations in which you should contact a qualified technician.
            </p>

            <h2 id="locked-oven">Locked Gorenje Oven – Why It Happens and How to Unlock It</h2>
            <p>
              When faced with a locked oven door or an unresponsive control panel, the first and most important rule is not to force the appliance open. Doing so could damage the locking mechanism, hinges or glass panel, making the subsequent repair more expensive. Depending on your oven model, the locking function is usually activated for one of the following three reasons.
            </p>

            <h3>Activated Child Lock</h3>
            <p>
              This is an extremely useful feature that helps prevent accidents in households with young children. When it is active, a key symbol or the message "Loc", short for "Locked", usually appears on the display.
            </p>
            <p>To deactivate the child lock successfully, follow these steps:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>For touchscreen displays:</strong> Press and hold the button with the key symbol for approximately three to five seconds. You should hear an audible signal, and the indicator on the display should disappear.</li>
              <li><strong>For models with combined controls:</strong> Some models require two specific buttons to be pressed and held simultaneously for several seconds. For example, this may involve the timer button and the temperature reduction button. Consult the user manual for your specific model to find the correct button combination.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Door Locked After a Pyrolytic Cleaning Programme</h3>
            <p>
              Pyrolysis is a self-cleaning function that operates at extremely high temperatures, reaching up to 500°C. For safety reasons, the oven automatically locks the door as soon as the process begins and keeps it locked until the interior has cooled to a safe temperature, usually below 150°C.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>The solution:</strong> The only correct action in this situation is to wait. Do not pull the door handle. Allow the appliance to cool completely. This usually takes between 30 and 90 minutes after the programme has finished. Once the temperature has dropped sufficiently, the electronic lock should release automatically.
            </p>

            <h3>Temporary System or Software Error</h3>
            <p>
              The oven's electronics may occasionally become unresponsive following a sudden power cut or an electrical surge.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>The solution:</strong> Perform a software reset. Switch off the oven's circuit breaker at the electrical panel for approximately 10 to 15 minutes. Turn it back on afterwards to restart the software. In many cases, the appliance will return to normal operation and the door will unlock automatically.
            </p>

            <h2 id="other-problems">Other Common Technical Problems with Gorenje Ovens</h2>
            <figure class="inline-article-image">
              <img src={doorImage} alt="Hand opening a silver oven door in a white kitchen" loading="lazy" />
            </figure>
            <p>
              In addition to questions about unlocking a Gorenje oven, users may also encounter genuine hardware faults. The following are some of the most common problems that may indicate the need for a professional diagnosis.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>The oven does not heat up, but the fan and light are working:</strong> This symptom often indicates a failed heating element. Ovens usually have an upper, lower and circular fan heating element. When the fan circulates air but the air remains cold, one of the heating elements may have failed. Another possible cause is a defective operating thermostat that is no longer sending the correct signal to activate the heating elements.</li>
              <li><strong>The display is flashing or does not light up at all:</strong> When the appliance has power but the display is dark or constantly flashing, the problem may be related to the control module, also known as the electronic circuit board, or the oven's power supply unit. Sometimes, after a power cut, the display is simply waiting for the clock to be reset. Many Gorenje models will not start a cooking programme until the correct time has been set.</li>
              <li><strong>Food cooks unevenly:</strong> If food burns on one side but remains undercooked on the other, the problem may be caused by a worn or torn rubber door seal that allows hot air to escape. Another possible cause is a fault in the convection fan motor or a build-up of burnt residue on the fan blades, which prevents the air from circulating properly.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 id="home-checks">What You Can Safely Check at Home</h2>
            <p>Before requesting professional assistance, there are several quick and completely safe checks you can carry out yourself:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Check the power supply:</strong> Make sure that the relevant circuit breaker in the electrical panel has not tripped. For freestanding appliances, test the wall socket with another appliance. Built-in ovens are usually connected directly to a terminal block inside the wall, so you should only check the corresponding circuit breaker in the electrical panel.</li>
              <li><strong>Set the clock on the display:</strong> If the display shows flashing numbers, such as "00:00", or a time symbol, set the correct time according to the user manual. The oven may not begin heating until this initial setting has been completed.</li>
              <li><strong>Clean the touchscreen control panel:</strong> Grease, moisture or dust on the touch controls may occasionally interfere with their operation and make the appliance appear locked. Wipe the panel with a slightly damp microfibre cloth and dry it thoroughly.</li>
              <li><strong>Make sure the door is fully closed:</strong> Many modern ovens have a built-in microswitch that prevents the appliance from operating when the door is not completely closed or when the seal is out of position.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 id="stop-using">When You Should Stop Using the Appliance Immediately</h2>
            <p>Safety in your kitchen should always come first. Stop using the oven immediately and switch off its circuit breaker at the electrical panel if you notice any of the following signs:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>A smell of burnt plastic or Bakelite:</strong> This may indicate overheating wires, a loose connection in the terminal block or damage to the control board.</li>
              <li><strong>Sparks or smoke:</strong> Switch off the corresponding circuit breaker immediately. Do not touch the appliance's metal casing.</li>
              <li><strong>The circuit breaker repeatedly trips:</strong> If the circuit breaker or residual-current device, also known as an RCD, trips as soon as you switch on the oven or select a particular programme, the appliance may have a short circuit or current leakage to the casing.</li>
              <li><strong>A loud metallic or grinding noise:</strong> This usually indicates worn bearings in the hot-air fan or the fan responsible for cooling the appliance casing. Continuing to use the oven in this condition may cause the motor to seize and the appliance to overheat.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 id="professional-repair">Professional Gorenje Oven Repair in Sofia and Varna</h2>
            <p>
              When the problem goes beyond adjusting the child lock or resetting the clock, the assistance of a qualified professional is essential. Attempting to dismantle the appliance, replace heating elements or repair electronic circuit boards yourself carries a serious risk of electric shock, fire and permanent damage to the oven.
            </p>
            <p>
              RotoRem provides on-site oven repair services for customers in Sofia, Varna and the surrounding areas. We offer <a href="https://www.rotorem.bg/en/services/sofia/oven-repair/" class="inline-link">oven repair in Sofia</a> and <a href="https://www.rotorem.bg/en/services/oven-repair/" class="inline-link">oven repair in Varna</a>. The technicians carry out a diagnosis at your address and identify the possible cause of the fault before recommending an appropriate solution.
            </p>
            <p>
              When a component needs to be replaced, compatible spare parts are selected according to the oven model and the specific fault.
            </p>

            <div class="cta-inline" role="group" aria-label="Contact options">
